{
  "dom_id": "step-q105470",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2}$ is continuous.",
  "worked_solution": "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-2)}{x-2} \\\\  & =x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 2.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals.",
